# What `deus view` prints

`deus view` prints a stored object as source that compiles back to the same hash
([CODEBASE-TEXT.md](../../docs/spec/CODEBASE-TEXT.md), D63), under a header naming its type. Two places printed what the
author never wrote ([D108](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)): a theorem's hypotheses came out as `prim "implies" (P) (Q)`,
which is Core, not source, and a type named by an IRI with a path (`urn:deus:task:Task`) came out as its
path (`task:Task`) in the type header.

Written in the order of the process ([D92](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)): requirements, then the APIs and laws, then
the implementation. Three of the seven theorems ran red before the fix; the round trip already held, since `prim "implies" P Q` is source too. `deus mdl View.Spec`
checks the blocks; `deus check View.Spec` runs the theorems.

## Extensions

| Term | URI |
| --- | --- |
| `api` | `org.dxos.deus.api@1.0` |
| `law` | `org.dxos.deus.law@1.0` |
| `req` | `org.dxos.deus.req@1.0` |

## APIs

```mdl
api A-1: printing theorems and types
  signatures:
  - View.theoremDecl : Printer -> Text -> List Sexp -> Sexp -> Sexp -> Text
  - Types.iriName : Text -> Text
  - Types.displayScheme : Sexp -> Text
  - Types.declaredNames : Map Text Entry -> Map Text Text
  - Types.displaySchemeNamed : Map Text Text -> Sexp -> Text
```

```mdl
api A-2: View definitions other namespaces use
  signatures:
  - View.abilityDecl : Printer -> Map Text Entry -> Text -> Entry -> Sexp -> Option Sexp -> Text
  - View.fieldNamesFor : Option Sexp -> List (List Text)
  - View.nameParams : List Text -> Sexp -> Sexp
  - View.paramsFor : Option Sexp -> Int -> Sexp -> List Text
  - View.typeArgs : Sexp -> List Sexp
  - View.typeDecl : Printer -> Map Text Entry -> Text -> Entry -> Sexp -> Option Sexp -> Text
```

## Theorems with hypotheses

A theorem's statement is `P₁ -> … -> Pₙ -> Q` at its top level only: the parser reads `->` there and
nowhere else, and stores it as `(app (prim "implies") P rest)`. The printer writes that chain back with
`->`, each part printed as a whole expression.

```mdl
req R-1: a theorem's hypotheses print as P -> Q, in order
  by: View.Spec.oneHypothesis | View.Spec.twoHypotheses
```

```mdl
law L-1: a printed theorem with hypotheses compiles back to the same hash
  theorem: View.Spec.hypothesesRoundTrip
  evidence: proof
```

## Type names in headers

The header (`-- : T`) prints a stored scheme, which names types by IRI. A builtin `#T` prints as `T`;
any other IRI prints as its last segment after `/`, `:` and `.`.

```mdl
req R-2: a type in a header prints as the last segment of its IRI
  by: View.Spec.pathIri | View.Spec.plainIri | View.Spec.webIri | View.Spec.builtinIri
```

Where the header knows the codebase (`deus view`, and `MdlHost.describe` behind an api block), a type or
ability prints by the name it is declared under: `MdlHost` is declared with the IRI `urn:deus:mdl:Host`, and
its last segment would print `Host`, a name nothing declares. An IRI no name declares falls back to R-2.

```mdl
req R-3: with the codebase at hand, a type or ability prints by its declared name, not its IRI's last segment
  by: View.Spec.abilityByName | View.Spec.typeByName | View.Spec.undeclaredByIri
```

Core has no names or annotations, so `def A.int (n : Int) : Int := n` and `def A.text (t : Text) : Text := t`
share a hash, and the hash's scheme generalizes both (D116) to `a -> a`. Callers are checked against that
scheme, which is the body's own type, so nothing unsound passes. A header, though, shows what its name
declares (task dda00bb4).

```mdl
req R-4: a name whose body other names share shows its own declared type, not the shared scheme
  by: View.Spec.sharedBodyShowsItsOwnType
```

## Theorems

The laws are the definitions under `View.Spec`: `deus ls View.Spec.` lists them and `deus view` prints them.
